// `runtime fn`s over data: `import runtime_data;` is enough to write one
// over a `Bytes(n)` span, a `laid record`, or an array field of one.
//
// `import runtime;` is enough for a `runtime fn` of words. A function of a
// span or a laid record publishes theorems that cite the span and record
// meanings (`rt_bytes`, `rt_held` and their loops) and the codecs'
// accessors (`stored_accessor`, `stored_words`), and the generator finds
// them by their qualified names in the importer's scope
// (`runtime_held_ref`, `runtime_bytes_ref` in `runtime.hthr`). Those
// modules cannot sit under `runtime`: `stored_accessor` and `stored_words`
// are themselves written with `runtime fn`, so they import it. This module
// sits above them all.
//
// Only what a function's source writes is exported: `runtime`'s words and
// spans, the `laid` generator, and `stored_words`, whose `Vec` methods are
// an array field's elements (`v.at(i)`, `v.put(i, x)`). A method is found
// in the modules the importer reaches through exports, not in its whole
// closure, so these three are exported; everything the generator cites is
// reached by its qualified name, so the rest is imported plainly and keeps
// its names out of the importer's scope.
import stored;
import part;
import layout;
import rt_held;
import rt_held_fields;
import rt_held_loop;
import rt_held_counted;
import stored_accessor;
import bytes;
import rt_bytes;
import rt_bytes_loop;
export import runtime;
export import laid;
export import stored_words;
